            <ArticleTitle>Intrauterine lignocaine as an anaesthetic during endometrial sampling: a randomised double-blind controlled trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>53-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the effectiveness of intrauterine lignocaine as an anaesthetic during endometrial sampling.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient gynaecological minor operation unit in a public hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two hundred premenopausal women scheduled for endometrial sampling because of abnormal uterine bleeding.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Transcervical intrauterine instillation of 5 mL of 2% lignocaine or 5 mL of normal saline before performing endometrial sampling with vacuum aspirator.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Evaluation of pain associated with the procedure using a visual analogue scale.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The use of intrauterine lignocaine reduced pain during suction curettage in endometrial sampling.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Transcervical instillation of lignocaine reduced pain during endometrial sampling.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
